 location="Begins at the level of the upper border of the thyroid cartilage (around C4) as the larger terminal branch of the common carotid; a carotid sinus dilatation marks its origin. The cervical segment gives no branches. The petrous segment turns forward and medially inside the temporal bone, the cavernous segment runs horizontally in the cavernous sinus lateral to the sphenoid body, and the supraclinoid segment lies in the subarachnoid space beside the optic chiasm and terminates at the medial end of the lateral sulcus.",
 subdivisions=[("Cervical", "no branches; the carotid sinus and body sit at the origin"), ("Petrous", "in the carotid canal, surrounded by the sympathetic carotid plexus"), ("Cavernous", "S-shaped siphon inside the cavernous sinus; the abducens nerve lies lateral to it"), ("Supraclinoid (cerebral)", "subarachnoid; gives ophthalmic, posterior communicating, anterior choroidal branches, then bifurcates")],
