     "On sagittal T1 the pericallosal artery is a curving flow void just above the corpus callosum; on axial slices the paired A2 segments sit in the midline in front of the genu. CTA and MRA show the A1 segments converging on the anterior communicating complex.",
     [pathol("ACA territory infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion along the medial frontal and parietal cortex from the frontal pole to the paracentral lobule, sometimes involving the caudate head and anterior limb of the capsule when the proximal A1/Heubner is included; bilateral when both A2s arise from one A1 (azygos variant).", sequence="DWI", timing="DWI bright within an hour; FLAIR follows by roughly 6 hours"),
      pathol("Anterior communicating artery aneurysm", "CTA", "Saccular aneurysm at the A1–A2 junction; rupture gives blood in the interhemispheric fissure and frontal horns with a gyrus rectus hematoma.", sequence="CT contrast")],
